INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A GenAI application uses Amazon Bedrock for text generation, experiencing high token costs for frequently asked questions. The responses are largely static for identical prompts. The development team needs to reduce inference costs and improve response times for these common queries. Which solution is the MOST cost-effective and performant?', '["Implement a deterministic caching layer using Amazon DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) to store prompt-response pairs.","Configure Amazon CloudFront to cache responses from Amazon Bedrock, which is not designed for dynamic GenAI responses.","Use AWS Lambda to preprocess prompts and filter out common keywords before sending to Amazon Bedrock, which adds complexity but doesn''t cache responses.","Increase the provisioned throughput for the Amazon Bedrock model to handle more requests concurrently, which will reduce individual response times but not address static content costs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) involves implementing a deterministic caching layer. For frequently asked questions with static responses, caching the exact prompt-response pairs is highly effective. Services like Amazon DynamoDB or Amazon ElastiCache can be used to store these pairs, significantly reducing the number of Amazon Bedrock invocations and improving response times. Option B is incorrect because Amazon CloudFront is primarily a CDN for static and dynamic web content, not designed for caching complex GenAI model responses directly. Option C would add preprocessing logic but does not directly cache the model''s output for identical prompts. Option D would increase performance by allowing more concurrent requests but would also increase costs, not reduce them for static content.', 67, 'm_67_1', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A startup is building a GenAI application that requires both high-quality, complex reasoning for critical business decisions and fast, low-cost responses for general user interactions. They currently use a single large model for all requests, leading to high costs. Which approach optimizes for cost-effectiveness while maintaining appropriate response quality?', '["Use a single, large foundation model with provisioned throughput to guarantee performance for all requests, which would be very expensive for general interactions.","Implement a model tiering strategy, using a smaller, cost-effective model for general interactions and a larger, more capable model for complex reasoning tasks.","Cache all responses from the large foundation model using Amazon ElastiCache to reduce repeated invocations, which only addresses repeated prompts, not the cost of varied requests.","Reduce the maximum output tokens for all model invocations to lower overall costs, which could compromise response quality for complex tasks."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) describes a model tiering strategy. By routing requests to different models based on their complexity and required quality, the application can leverage smaller, more cost-effective models for simpler tasks and reserve larger, more expensive models for critical, complex reasoning. This directly addresses the cost-capability tradeoff. Option A is incorrect because using a single large model for all requests is not cost-effective for simple interactions. Option C helps with repeated prompts but doesn''t optimize the cost of unique invocations. Option D might reduce costs but risks degrading the quality of responses, especially for complex tasks.', 67, 'm_67_1', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A customer support GenAI agent processes long user queries and internal knowledge base articles to generate comprehensive responses. The current approach sends the full context to the LLM, resulting in high token usage and increased latency. Which solution reduces token usage and associated costs without losing critical information?', '["Increase the model''s context window on Amazon Bedrock to accommodate longer inputs, which would not reduce token usage or cost.","Store all knowledge base articles in Amazon S3 and retrieve them on demand during model invocation, without addressing token efficiency for the prompt itself.","Implement a prompt compression technique, such as summarization or query rewriting, before sending the full context to the foundation model.","Use a larger, more powerful foundation model that can handle longer prompts more efficiently, but at a higher cost per token."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is to implement a prompt compression technique. This involves reducing the length of the input prompt or context by summarizing irrelevant parts or rewriting queries to be more concise, thereby decreasing token usage and the associated costs and latency. Option A is incorrect because increasing the context window doesn''t reduce token usage; it merely allows more tokens. Option B is a retrieval strategy but doesn''t inherently compress the prompt sent to the LLM. Option D might handle longer prompts but typically comes with a higher cost per token, counteracting the goal of cost reduction.', 67, 'm_67_1', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'An analytics company uses a GenAI model on Amazon Bedrock to process daily reports, generating summaries and insights. The workload is predictable, with a large volume of documents processed once a day during off-peak hours. Real-time latency is not critical for this specific task. Which approach minimizes inference costs for the predictable, high-volume batch workload?', '["Configure a real-time API Gateway endpoint for Amazon Bedrock and send documents one by one, incurring higher per-request overhead.","Increase the provisioned throughput for the Amazon Bedrock model during the processing window to ensure fast individual responses, which is not cost-optimal for batch.","Use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream documents directly to Amazon Bedrock for continuous processing, which is designed for real-time streaming, not cost-optimized batch.","Implement a batch processing workflow using AWS Step Functions to orchestrate sending multiple documents in a single Amazon Bedrock request, leveraging batch pricing where available."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) suggests implementing a batch processing workflow. For predictable, high-volume, non-real-time workloads, batching requests to Amazon Bedrock can significantly reduce inference costs. AWS Step Functions can orchestrate this workflow, collecting documents and sending them in optimized batches. Option A is incorrect because real-time, one-by-one processing is expensive for batch workloads. Option B would increase costs by provisioning dedicated throughput, which is unnecessary given the non-real-time constraint. Option C is designed for continuous, real-time data ingestion, not cost-optimized batch processing.', 67, 'm_67_1', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A content generation platform uses a large language model via Amazon Bedrock to create marketing copy. Users often submit slightly rephrased prompts that convey the same intent, leading to redundant model invocations and higher costs. Which solution reduces redundant model invocations and costs for semantically similar prompts?', '["Use a semantic caching strategy where prompt embeddings are compared, and cached responses are returned for semantically similar prompts.","Implement a deterministic caching layer to store exact prompt-response pairs in Amazon DynamoDB, which would not catch semantically similar but not identical prompts.","Train a custom model on Amazon SageMaker to identify and merge semantically similar prompts before sending them to Amazon Bedrock, which adds significant operational overhead.","Reduce the model''s `temperature` parameter to make responses more predictable and thus more cacheable, but this doesn''t address semantic similarity directly."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is to implement a semantic caching strategy. This approach involves generating embeddings for incoming prompts and comparing them against embeddings of previously cached prompts. If a new prompt is semantically similar to a cached one, the cached response can be returned, significantly reducing redundant model invocations and costs. Option B is incorrect because deterministic caching only works for exact prompt matches, failing to address semantically similar but rephrased prompts. Option C introduces significant development and operational overhead for a task that can be handled more efficiently with a caching layer. Option D might make responses more consistent but doesn''t directly enable caching for semantically similar inputs.', 67, 'm_67_1', 5, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A GenAI application is designed to summarize long articles. The current implementation passes the entire article to the LLM, which often exceeds context window limits for larger articles and incurs high token costs. Which approach improves token efficiency and ensures all relevant information is processed for summarization?', '["Configure Amazon CloudFront to cache the summarized output of the GenAI model, which does not address the input token limit or cost.","Split the article into smaller chunks, summarize each chunk, and then combine the summaries using a final model invocation.","Request a service limit increase for the maximum input token size for the Amazon Bedrock model, which is generally not a sustainable or cost-effective solution for large articles.","Use a different foundation model that has a larger context window, regardless of its cost, which may not be the most cost-effective solution."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) describes a recursive summarization approach. By splitting long articles into smaller chunks, summarizing each chunk individually, and then combining these summaries for a final summarization, the application can handle articles of arbitrary length while staying within the model''s context window and managing token costs more effectively. Option A is incorrect as caching the output doesn''t solve the input token limit issue. Option C is a temporary workaround and not a scalable architectural solution. Option D might work but could be significantly more expensive if the larger model is not justified for all summarization tasks.', 67, 'm_67_1', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A development team is building a new GenAI feature for a mobile application that provides quick, concise answers to common user questions. The current implementation uses a powerful, expensive foundation model for all responses, leading to high operational costs. Which solution achieves the MOST cost-effective outcome for simple queries without compromising user experience?', '["Implement a comprehensive prompt engineering strategy to make the expensive model more efficient for simple queries, which still incurs the high cost of the powerful model.","Use Amazon CloudFront to cache all responses from the expensive foundation model, reducing the number of invocations, but not addressing the cost per invocation for non-cached items.","Switch to a smaller, more specialized, and less expensive foundation model for handling simple, common user questions, while reserving the powerful model for complex requests.","Increase the provisioned throughput of the expensive model to improve response times, thus justifying its cost, which would further increase costs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is to implement a model tiering strategy by switching to a smaller, less expensive foundation model for simple queries. This allows the application to leverage the cost-effectiveness of specialized models for common tasks, significantly reducing operational costs while maintaining acceptable response quality and speed for those specific interactions. The more powerful, expensive model can be reserved for complex requests where its capabilities are truly needed. Option A, while useful, doesn''t fundamentally reduce the cost per token of the expensive model. Option B helps with repeated queries but not the underlying cost of unique simple queries. Option D would increase costs, not reduce them.', 67, 'm_67_1', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A financial analytics firm is developing a Generative AI application that summarizes market reports. To minimize inference costs, the solution architect needs to reduce the number of tokens processed by the large language model (LLM) for frequently asked queries. The summaries generated must be consistent for identical inputs. Which approach should the architect implement to achieve the MOST cost-effective token usage?', '["Implement a deterministic caching layer using Amazon DynamoDB to store previous LLM responses for identical prompts.","Use a prompt compression technique like summarization or keyword extraction before sending prompts to the LLM.","Employ a model tiering strategy, routing simple queries to smaller, less expensive LLMs.","Configure AWS Lambda to batch multiple user requests into a single LLM invocation to amortize costs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most cost-effective approach for reducing token usage for frequently asked, identical queries while ensuring consistent responses. A deterministic caching layer, such as one implemented with Amazon DynamoDB, stores the LLM''s output for a given input. If the same prompt is received again, the cached response is returned without invoking the LLM, directly saving inference costs and tokens. Option B (incorrect) involves prompt compression, which can reduce input tokens but still requires LLM invocation and might alter the original prompt''s intent or lose context, and it doesn''t guarantee consistent responses for identical inputs if the compression algorithm varies or if the LLM itself is non-deterministic. Option C (incorrect) is a valid cost optimization strategy for model tiering but focuses on routing different types of queries to different models, not specifically on reducing token usage for *identical* and *frequent* queries. It also doesn''t guarantee consistency in the same way a cache does. Option D (incorrect) is a valid strategy for optimizing provisioned throughput or batching, which can reduce the per-token cost by processing multiple requests together, but it still incurs the token cost for each request and doesn''t avoid LLM invocation for identical, cached prompts.', 67, 'm_67_1', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A media company is developing a Generative AI application that summarizes long articles. The application needs to process a high volume of articles daily, but the LLM inference costs are becoming prohibitive. The summaries do not need to be real-time for all articles; some can be processed with a slight delay. The architect needs to implement a cost-effective solution for LLM inference that optimizes resource utilization. Which strategy should be employed?', '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform to process articles in large, scheduled batches, leveraging cost-effective instance types.","Deploy the LLM on Amazon EC2 instances with Auto Scaling, scaling up during peak hours and down during off-peak hours.","Implement a model tiering strategy, routing short articles to a smaller, less expensive LLM and long articles to a more capable LLM.","Configure AWS Lambda to invoke the LLM for each article, optimizing for serverless execution and automatic scaling."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most cost-effective strategy for processing a high volume of articles where real-time responses are not strictly required for all. Amazon SageMaker Batch Transform is designed for offline inference on large datasets. It allows processing articles in large batches, which significantly improves resource utilization and reduces per-inference costs compared to real-time endpoints. It also supports using various instance types, including those optimized for cost. Option B (incorrect) involves deploying on Amazon EC2 with Auto Scaling. While this provides flexibility, managing EC2 instances and the associated scaling logic still incurs more operational overhead and potentially higher costs per inference compared to Batch Transform for non-real-time workloads. Option C (incorrect) describes a model tiering strategy, which is a valid cost optimization, but it focuses on routing based on article length/complexity, not on leveraging batch processing for overall cost reduction for the *entire volume* of articles, especially when delays are acceptable. Option D (incorrect) suggests using AWS Lambda for individual invocations. While serverless, invoking an LLM for each article, especially long ones, can be expensive due to token usage and the duration of Lambda functions. Batch processing is generally more cost-effective for high-volume, non-real-time workloads.', 67, 'm_67_1', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A marketing agency uses a Generative AI model to create ad copy. They frequently submit prompts that are very similar but not identical, leading to repeated LLM invocations and higher costs. The agency wants to reduce these inference costs by leveraging previous responses for semantically similar prompts, while maintaining the quality and relevance of the generated copy. Which cost optimization strategy should the architect recommend?', '["Implement a deterministic caching layer using Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to store exact prompt-response pairs.","Utilize prompt engineering techniques to standardize prompts, making them identical for caching purposes.","Employ a semantic caching layer that stores embeddings of prompts and their responses, serving cached results for new, semantically similar prompts.","Configure Amazon SageMaker to use a smaller, fine-tuned LLM for all ad copy generation to reduce per-token costs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective strategy for reducing inference costs when prompts are ''very similar but not identical''. A semantic caching layer works by converting prompts into embeddings and storing these embeddings along with their corresponding LLM responses. When a new prompt arrives, its embedding is compared for semantic similarity against the cached embeddings. If a sufficiently similar match is found, the cached response is returned, avoiding a new LLM invocation and significantly reducing token usage and costs. This approach directly addresses the challenge of similar but not identical prompts. Option A (incorrect) suggests a deterministic caching layer, which only works for *exact* prompt matches. Since the prompts are ''very similar but not identical'', a deterministic cache would miss most opportunities for cost savings. Option B (incorrect) suggests prompt engineering to standardize prompts. While this can help, it requires significant manual effort and might limit the flexibility and nuance needed for effective ad copy generation, potentially compromising quality. It''s also not an automated architectural solution. Option D (incorrect) proposes using a smaller, fine-tuned LLM. While this can reduce per-token costs, it still incurs an LLM invocation for every request and does not leverage previous responses for similar prompts, thus missing a significant optimization opportunity for recurring, similar requests.', 67, 'm_67_1', 10, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_67_3
