INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A company has deployed a GenAI application on AWS that uses Amazon Bedrock. They need to track token consumption for different user segments and detect sudden spikes in usage that could indicate cost anomalies or potential abuse. Which monitoring solution provides granular token usage visibility and alerts for cost anomalies?', '["Configure Amazon CloudWatch metrics and alarms based on Amazon Bedrock invocation logs, enriched with token usage data, and integrate with Amazon SNS for notifications.","Use AWS Cost Explorer to analyze daily spending and manually set up email alerts for budget overruns, which lacks real-time granular token usage.","Implement a custom logging solution within the application to count tokens and send data to Amazon S3 for weekly analysis, which is not real-time and adds operational overhead.","Rely solely on AWS Budgets to monitor the overall AWS account spending, without specific GenAI token metrics, which is not granular enough for token usage."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most effective solution. Amazon Bedrock invocation logs can be configured to capture detailed information, including token usage. By sending these logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, custom metrics can be extracted and used to create CloudWatch Alarms. These alarms can then trigger Amazon SNS notifications for cost anomalies or usage spikes, providing granular, real-time visibility. Option B offers high-level cost analysis but lacks the real-time, granular token usage data. Option C requires significant custom development and doesn''t provide real-time alerting. Option D provides overall budget monitoring but not specific GenAI token usage metrics.', 67, 'm_67_3', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A GenAI-powered chatbot provides customer support, and the development team is concerned about the accuracy and relevance of its responses, particularly the occurrence of hallucinations. They need a system to continuously evaluate response quality and identify potential issues. Which mechanism effectively assesses response quality and detects hallucinations?', '["Implement an automated system using AWS Lambda to compare model responses against a predefined set of correct answers for all prompts, which is difficult for open-ended GenAI responses.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch alarms to trigger when the model''s output token count deviates significantly from the average, which is not an indicator of quality or hallucination.","Use AWS X-Ray to trace the model invocation path and identify any errors during response generation, which focuses on technical errors, not content quality.","Capture model responses and implement a human-in-the-loop feedback mechanism, leveraging services like Amazon S3 for storage and AWS Step Functions for workflow orchestration, to rate response quality."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most effective method for assessing response quality and detecting hallucinations in GenAI applications. A human-in-the-loop feedback mechanism allows human evaluators to review model responses, rate their quality, and identify instances of hallucination or inaccuracy. Services like Amazon S3 can store responses, and AWS Step Functions can orchestrate the review workflow. Option A is impractical for the subjective and varied nature of GenAI responses. Option B monitors token count, which doesn''t correlate directly with response quality or hallucination. Option C focuses on technical tracing, not content evaluation.', 67, 'm_67_3', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A media company is building a GenAI application to generate personalized news articles. They need to monitor the application''s performance, specifically focusing on latency and throughput, to ensure a smooth user experience. The solution must also provide insights into the cost implications of different model invocation patterns. Which combination of AWS services should the architect use to meet these requirements with high granularity?', '["Utilize Amazon CloudWatch for custom metrics on token usage and response times, and Amazon S3 for storing raw invocation logs.","Implement Amazon CloudWatch custom metrics for latency and throughput, and enable Amazon SageMaker Model Invocation Logging to Amazon CloudWatch Logs for detailed token usage and cost analysis.","Deploy AWS X-Ray to trace requests across the application components, and Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream invocation logs to Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time analytics.","Configure AWS Trusted Advisor for cost optimization recommendations, and Amazon EventBridge for alerting on high latency thresholds."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) directly addresses the requirements for monitoring latency, throughput, token usage, and cost implications with high granularity. Amazon CloudWatch allows the creation of custom metrics for latency and throughput, providing real-time visibility into application performance. Enabling Amazon SageMaker Model Invocation Logging sends detailed information, including token usage and invocation details, to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. This data can then be analyzed using CloudWatch Logs Insights or integrated with CloudWatch Dashboards to derive cost implications per invocation and track token consumption patterns. Option A (incorrect) suggests S3 for raw logs, which is suitable for archival but less ideal for real-time, granular analysis of invocation patterns and cost. CloudWatch custom metrics are good, but S3 doesn''t provide the immediate analytical capabilities for logs that CloudWatch Logs does. Option C (incorrect) uses AWS X-Ray for tracing, which is excellent for identifying bottlenecks across distributed components, but it doesn''t inherently provide detailed token usage or direct cost analysis per invocation. Streaming logs to OpenSearch Service is powerful for analytics but adds complexity and cost compared to leveraging CloudWatch Logs directly for invocation data. Option D (incorrect) uses AWS Trusted Advisor for cost recommendations, which is a high-level service, not for granular, real-time monitoring of GenAI application performance or token usage. Amazon EventBridge is for event-driven architectures and alerting, not for collecting detailed performance metrics or invocation logs.', 67, 'm_67_3', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A healthcare provider is building a Generative AI application to assist doctors in drafting patient notes. The application needs robust monitoring to detect anomalies in response quality, such as hallucination rates, and to track token usage for cost allocation. The solution must provide actionable insights and allow for custom dashboards. Which set of AWS services should the architect choose to meet these requirements with LEAST operational overhead?', '["Configure Amazon CloudWatch for custom metrics on token usage and response quality, and integrate Amazon SageMaker Model Invocation Logging with CloudWatch Logs for detailed analysis.","Implement AWS X-Ray for tracing API calls to the LLM, and use Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream logs to an Amazon OpenSearch Service cluster for real-time dashboards.","Store LLM invocation logs in Amazon S3, and use AWS Glue and Amazon Athena for ad-hoc querying and reporting on token usage and response quality.","Develop a custom logging solution on an Amazon EC2 instance to capture LLM inputs/outputs, and use Grafana for visualization."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the most effective solution with the LEAST operational overhead. Amazon CloudWatch is a fully managed monitoring service that can collect custom metrics for token usage and response quality (e.g., by analyzing output for specific keywords or patterns indicative of hallucination). Integrating Amazon SageMaker Model Invocation Logging with CloudWatch Logs automatically captures detailed invocation data, which can then be analyzed using CloudWatch Logs Insights or visualized in CloudWatch Dashboards. This approach leverages fully managed services, minimizing the need for infrastructure management. Option B (incorrect) involves AWS X-Ray for tracing, which is good for performance but doesn''t directly track hallucination rates or token usage for cost allocation. Using Kinesis Data Firehose and Amazon OpenSearch Service adds significant operational overhead for managing the OpenSearch cluster and data ingestion pipeline, which is more complex than CloudWatch''s integrated logging and metrics. Option C (incorrect) uses Amazon S3 for logs, and AWS Glue/Athena for querying. While viable, this approach is better suited for batch analysis and ad-hoc queries, not real-time monitoring or custom dashboards with the same ease and low overhead as CloudWatch. It also requires managing Glue jobs. Option D (incorrect) requires developing and managing a custom logging solution on Amazon EC2, which introduces substantial operational overhead for server management, scaling, security, and maintenance, directly violating the ''LEAST operational overhead'' constraint.', 67, 'm_67_3', 14, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A research institution is developing a Generative AI application that processes sensitive scientific papers. They need to monitor for potential data leakage or misuse by tracking all interactions with the LLM, including prompts and generated completions. This monitoring must be auditable and allow for retrospective analysis to identify any unauthorized access patterns or unusual token usage. Which AWS service combination provides this capability with high fidelity?', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs to capture all application logs, and Amazon CloudWatch Metrics for LLM invocation counts.","Configure AWS CloudTrail to log API calls to the LLM endpoint, and Amazon S3 to store application-level prompt and completion data.","Enable Amazon SageMaker Model Invocation Logging to Amazon CloudWatch Logs for detailed prompt and completion capture, and use AWS CloudTrail for API call auditing.","Implement AWS Config to track changes to the LLM endpoint configuration, and Amazon GuardDuty for threat detection."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) provides the most comprehensive and high-fidelity solution for monitoring sensitive LLM interactions for data leakage and misuse. Amazon SageMaker Model Invocation Logging, when enabled, captures the full prompt and completion data, along with other invocation details, and sends it to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. This is crucial for retrospective analysis of actual data processed by the LLM. AWS CloudTrail, on the other hand, logs all API calls made to AWS services, including the SageMaker LLM endpoint, providing an auditable trail of who accessed the model and when. Together, these services offer both the content of the interaction and the audit trail of access, which is essential for security and compliance. Option A (incorrect) uses CloudWatch Logs for general application logs and CloudWatch Metrics for counts, but it doesn''t explicitly capture the detailed prompt and completion content from the LLM invocation itself. Option B (incorrect) suggests CloudTrail for API calls (which is good) and Amazon S3 for application-level data. While S3 can store data, it requires custom implementation to capture prompt/completion data and lacks the integrated analysis capabilities of CloudWatch Logs for invocation data. Option D (incorrect) uses AWS Config for configuration changes and GuardDuty for threat detection. While valuable for security, neither directly captures the content of LLM prompts and completions or provides a direct audit trail of LLM interactions in the way SageMaker Model Invocation Logging and CloudTrail do.', 67, 'm_67_3', 15, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A software development firm is building a GenAI code assistant. They need to monitor the assistant''s performance and identify potential issues like incorrect code suggestions or excessive resource consumption. The monitoring solution must track agent/tool performance and provide detailed logs for debugging. Which AWS services should be integrated to provide comprehensive observability for this application?', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch for application metrics and logs, and AWS X-Ray for distributed tracing across microservices.","Configure AWS Trusted Advisor for cost optimization, and Amazon EventBridge for event-driven alerts.","Store all application logs in Amazon S3, and use Amazon Athena for querying and generating reports.","Implement AWS Config for resource compliance, and Amazon GuardDuty for security threat detection."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides comprehensive observability for the GenAI code assistant. Amazon CloudWatch is essential for collecting application metrics (e.g., latency, error rates, token usage) and centralizing logs (e.g., detailed logs from the code assistant''s logic, agent actions, tool invocations, and LLM interactions). AWS X-Ray complements this by providing distributed tracing, allowing developers to visualize the entire request flow across different microservices or components of the code assistant, identify bottlenecks, and debug issues related to agent/tool performance. This combination offers both aggregated metrics, detailed logs, and end-to-end transaction visibility. Option B (incorrect) focuses on cost optimization (Trusted Advisor) and event-driven alerts (EventBridge), which are not primarily designed for comprehensive application performance monitoring, logging, or tracing of a GenAI application''s internal workings. Option C (incorrect) suggests storing logs in Amazon S3 and querying with Amazon Athena. While good for large-scale log archival and ad-hoc analysis, it lacks the real-time monitoring, metric collection, and integrated dashboarding capabilities of CloudWatch, and the tracing capabilities of X-Ray. Option D (incorrect) uses AWS Config for compliance and GuardDuty for security, which are important security and governance services but do not provide the necessary observability into application performance, logs, or agent/tool execution details.', 67, 'm_67_3', 16, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_67_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A content generation platform uses a large language model (LLM) to create marketing copy. Users are experiencing noticeable delays, and the platform needs to improve the perceived responsiveness of the application. The architect must implement a solution that reduces the time users wait for the complete output from the LLM, without significantly increasing infrastructure costs. Which performance optimization strategy is MOST appropriate?', '["Implement parallel processing of user requests by deploying multiple LLM instances behind an Application Load Balancer.","Enable response streaming from the LLM to the client application, allowing partial content to be displayed as it is generated.","Increase the provisioned throughput of the Amazon SageMaker endpoint hosting the LLM to handle more concurrent requests.","Optimize the retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) component to fetch context from Amazon OpenSearch Service more efficiently."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate strategy for improving the *perceived responsiveness* and reducing user wait time without significantly increasing infrastructure costs. Response streaming allows the application to display parts of the LLM''s output as soon as they are generated, rather than waiting for the entire response. This dramatically improves the user experience by providing immediate feedback, even if the total time for the LLM to generate the full response remains the same. Option A (incorrect) involves parallel processing with multiple LLM instances, which increases throughput but also directly increases infrastructure costs by requiring more resources. It doesn''t inherently reduce the time for a single LLM to generate its full response. Option C (incorrect) suggests increasing provisioned throughput, which is a direct cost increase and primarily addresses overall capacity and latency under high load, not necessarily the time for a single user to receive a complete response if the bottleneck is the LLM''s generation speed. Option D (incorrect) focuses on optimizing the RAG component. While important for overall latency, it only addresses the context retrieval phase. If the LLM generation itself is the bottleneck for perceived responsiveness, optimizing RAG alone will not fully solve the problem of users waiting for the complete output.', 67, 'm_67_2', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A research team is developing a Generative AI model for drug discovery, requiring extensive experimentation with various model architectures and hyperparameters. They need to rapidly iterate on models, measure their performance, and compare different configurations to identify the most effective ones. The primary goal is to accelerate the model development lifecycle by optimizing the time spent on performance benchmarking. Which approach should the architect recommend?', '["Utilize Amazon SageMaker Hyperparameter Tuning to automatically find the best set of hyperparameters for the model.","Implement a continuous integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipeline using AWS CodePipeline and AWS CodeBuild to automate model deployment.","Leverage Amazon SageMaker Experiments to track, compare, and analyze different model training runs and their performance metrics.","Deploy the models on Amazon EC2 instances with GPU acceleration and manually log performance metrics to a custom database."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective approach for accelerating the model development lifecycle by optimizing performance benchmarking. Amazon SageMaker Experiments is specifically designed to organize, track, compare, and evaluate machine learning experiments. It allows the research team to log all parameters, metrics, and artifacts for each model training run, making it easy to compare different model architectures, hyperparameters, and datasets. This capability directly supports rapid iteration and identification of the most effective models, streamlining the benchmarking process. Option A (incorrect) suggests Amazon SageMaker Hyperparameter Tuning, which is excellent for finding optimal hyperparameters but is a specific optimization technique, not a general solution for tracking and comparing *all* aspects of model development and performance across different architectures. Option B (incorrect) describes a CI/CD pipeline, which is crucial for automating deployment but does not directly address the need for tracking and comparing experimental results and performance metrics during the development phase. Option D (incorrect) involves deploying models on Amazon EC2 and manually logging metrics. This approach introduces significant operational overhead for managing EC2 instances and developing a custom logging solution, which is inefficient and error-prone compared to a managed service like SageMaker Experiments.', 67, 'm_67_2', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A company is developing a Generative AI application that uses a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) pattern. The application''s performance is critical, and the architect needs to ensure low latency for information retrieval from the knowledge base. The knowledge base is stored in Amazon OpenSearch Service. Which optimization technique should be applied to the RAG component to improve overall application latency?', '["Implement parallel processing of LLM requests to generate responses concurrently.","Optimize the embedding model used for vectorizing documents and queries to reduce embedding generation time.","Configure OpenSearch Service with appropriate instance types and scaling to handle query load efficiently and reduce retrieval latency.","Tune the LLM''s `max_new_tokens` parameter to generate shorter, more concise responses."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) directly addresses improving the latency of information retrieval from the knowledge base, which is a critical part of the RAG pattern. Configuring Amazon OpenSearch Service with appropriate instance types, scaling, and indexing strategies ensures that queries to the knowledge base are processed efficiently, reducing the time it takes to retrieve relevant context. This directly contributes to lower overall application latency. Option A (incorrect) focuses on parallel processing of LLM requests, which improves throughput but does not directly reduce the latency of the RAG component''s retrieval phase. Option B (incorrect) suggests optimizing the embedding model. While important for accuracy and potentially for the speed of embedding generation, the primary bottleneck for retrieval latency is often the search performance of the vector database (OpenSearch Service in this case), not solely the embedding generation time. Option D (incorrect) involves tuning the LLM''s `max_new_tokens` parameter, which affects the length of the generated response. This impacts the LLM''s generation time but does not optimize the retrieval latency from the knowledge base.', 67, 'm_67_2', 19, NULL, NULL);

