INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A development team is iterating on a new foundational model (FM) for content generation and needs to compare different model versions based on human preferences and fluency. They want to integrate human feedback into their CI/CD pipeline with the LEAST manual effort for data collection and aggregation. Which solution meets this requirement?', '["Set up a custom web application on Amazon EC2 for human annotators to rate model outputs, then manually export and process the ratings.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to create a human review workflow, where annotators compare outputs from different model versions, and integrate the output manifest files directly into the training pipeline.","Distribute model outputs via email to internal stakeholders for feedback, then manually compile their responses in a spreadsheet for analysis.","Implement an AWS Lambda function to generate synthetic human feedback based on predefined rules and metrics, storing it in Amazon S3."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) uses Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth, which is specifically designed for building high-quality training datasets and collecting human feedback efficiently. It provides built-in workflows for tasks like comparative review and integrates well with data pipelines, minimizing manual effort for data collection and aggregation. Option A (incorrect) requires significant development and operational overhead to build and maintain a custom web application on Amazon EC2, and manual export/processing of data, which increases manual effort. Option C (incorrect) is a highly inefficient and error-prone manual process for collecting and compiling feedback, completely failing the ''LEAST manual effort'' constraint. Option D (incorrect) generates synthetic feedback, which does not capture genuine human preferences and fluency, thus failing to meet the core requirement of comparing model versions based on actual human judgment.', 68, 'm_68_2', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'An e-commerce platform uses a GenAI model to generate product descriptions. They want to perform A/B testing to compare two different prompt engineering strategies on customer engagement metrics (e.g., click-through rates) while ensuring minimal operational overhead for experiment management. Which AWS service combination is MOST suitable?', '["Deploy each prompt strategy as a separate AWS Lambda function, use Amazon API Gateway for routing, and manually collect engagement metrics from application logs in Amazon CloudWatch.","Host both GenAI models on separate Amazon EC2 instances, use an Application Load Balancer for traffic distribution, and manually track metrics in a custom database.","Utilize Amazon SageMaker Endpoints with production variants, configuring traffic splitting between two model versions (each using a different prompt strategy), and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch for metric collection.","Implement a custom A/B testing framework within the application code, dynamically switching between prompt strategies based on user IDs, and store results in Amazon DynamoDB."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most suitable approach for A/B testing GenAI models with minimal operational overhead. Amazon SageMaker Endpoints natively support production variants, allowing traffic splitting between different model versions or configurations (like different prompt strategies). It also integrates seamlessly with Amazon CloudWatch for monitoring and metric collection, simplifying experiment management. Option A (incorrect) requires custom routing logic and manual metric collection, increasing operational overhead compared to SageMaker''s built-in capabilities. Option B (incorrect) involves managing EC2 instances, which introduces significant operational overhead for infrastructure management, scaling, and patching, contrary to the ''minimal operational overhead'' constraint. Option D (incorrect) places the burden of A/B testing framework development and management on the application code, which adds complexity, maintenance, and potential for errors, rather than leveraging a managed service.', 68, 'm_68_2', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A media company is fine-tuning a large language model (LLM) for summarization tasks. They want to automate the assessment of summarization quality, focusing on conciseness and factual consistency, by using another LLM as a judge. Which AWS services would best support this automated assessment?', '["Use Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth to have human annotators manually score each summary for conciseness and factual consistency.","Leverage Amazon Bedrock to host the fine-tuned LLM and a separate foundational model as an LLM-as-a-judge, orchestrating the evaluation logic with AWS Step Functions.","Deploy the fine-tuned LLM on Amazon EC2 and write custom Python scripts to calculate ROUGE scores for conciseness and BLEU scores for factual consistency.","Implement Amazon Comprehend to analyze the sentiment of the generated summaries, using sentiment as a proxy for quality."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) directly supports the requirement to automate assessment using an LLM-as-a-judge. Amazon Bedrock can host both the fine-tuned LLM for summarization and another foundational model to act as the judge, evaluating the summaries for conciseness and factual consistency. AWS Step Functions can orchestrate the entire evaluation workflow, making it automated and scalable. Option A (incorrect) relies on human annotators, which is not an automated assessment and would be costly and time-consuming for continuous evaluation. Option C (incorrect) involves managing EC2 instances and developing custom scoring scripts, which increases operational overhead and might not capture the nuanced quality aspects (like factual consistency) as effectively as an LLM-as-a-judge. Option D (incorrect) uses Amazon Comprehend for sentiment analysis, which is irrelevant to assessing summarization quality, conciseness, or factual consistency.', 68, 'm_68_2', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A gaming company is developing an AI NPC dialogue system. They need to evaluate the fluency and coherence of generated dialogue in a continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD) pipeline. The goal is to detect regressions in dialogue quality automatically before deployment. Which approach should they adopt?', '["Conduct manual playtesting sessions after every deployment to identify dialogue quality issues and report them to the development team.","Implement A/B testing in production, exposing a small percentage of users to new dialogue versions and collecting implicit feedback on engagement.","Set up an automated quality gate in AWS CodePipeline that invokes an AWS Lambda function to run an LLM-as-a-judge evaluation on a golden dataset of prompts, comparing fluency and coherence metrics against a baseline.","Use Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the number of API calls to the dialogue generation service, assuming a consistent call volume indicates good quality."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) provides an automated and proactive solution for detecting regressions in dialogue quality before deployment. By integrating an automated quality gate in AWS CodePipeline, an AWS Lambda function can trigger an LLM-as-a-judge evaluation against a golden dataset. This allows for objective and scalable assessment of fluency and coherence metrics, comparing them against established baselines to prevent regressions from reaching production. Option A (incorrect) relies on manual playtesting, which is not automated and would significantly slow down the CI/CD pipeline, failing to detect regressions automatically before deployment. Option B (incorrect) performs A/B testing in production, which means regressions could be exposed to users, and relies on implicit feedback, which is less direct for detecting specific fluency/coherence issues compared to a dedicated evaluation. Option D (incorrect) monitors API call volume, which is a usage metric and has no direct correlation with the quality (fluency and coherence) of the generated dialogue.', 68, 'm_68_2', 14, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A marketing team uses a GenAI model to create ad copy. They want to conduct A/B tests to determine which ad copy variations lead to higher conversion rates, and need a solution that allows dynamic routing of traffic to different model versions without downtime. Which AWS architecture is most appropriate?', '["Deploy each ad copy generation model version on separate Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer, manually updating target group weights for traffic distribution.","Host the GenAI model on an Amazon SageMaker Endpoint, using production variants to define different model versions (e.g., each with a distinct prompt engineering strategy) and configure traffic splitting for A/B testing.","Implement a custom traffic routing logic within an AWS Lambda function that invokes different GenAI models based on a random assignment, and store conversion data in Amazon RDS.","Use Amazon CloudFront to cache ad copy variations and distribute them globally, relying on its caching behavior to manage A/B testing traffic."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most appropriate and managed solution. Amazon SageMaker Endpoints with production variants are specifically designed for A/B testing and blue/green deployments of machine learning models. They allow dynamic traffic splitting between different model versions (which can represent different ad copy generation strategies) without downtime, and provide integrated monitoring. Option A (incorrect) involves managing EC2 instances, which adds significant operational overhead compared to a managed service like SageMaker, and manual updates to target group weights are less efficient for dynamic A/B testing. Option C (incorrect) requires building and maintaining custom traffic routing logic within AWS Lambda, which adds complexity and operational burden that SageMaker already handles natively. Option D (incorrect) uses Amazon CloudFront for caching and distribution, which is not designed for dynamic A/B testing of GenAI model outputs or for managing the underlying model inference logic.', 68, 'm_68_2', 15, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A company''s GenAI chatbot is experiencing ''prompt confusion'' where it misinterprets user intent. They need to implement a logging and monitoring solution to capture detailed prompt inputs and model outputs, allowing for post-hoc analysis of reasoning paths to understand misinterpretations. Which combination of services is best for this task?', '["Store all prompt inputs and model outputs in Amazon DynamoDB for quick retrieval, and use Amazon CloudWatch Metrics to count misinterpretations.","Configure Amazon CloudWatch Logs to capture all prompt inputs and model outputs, and use AWS X-Ray to trace the execution flow of the GenAI application components.","Use Amazon S3 to store raw prompt inputs and model outputs, and manually download and analyze logs periodically.","Implement Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream all prompt inputs and model outputs to Amazon Redshift for real-time analytics dashboards."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides a comprehensive logging and tracing solution. Amazon CloudWatch Logs is ideal for capturing detailed prompt inputs and model outputs, allowing for full text search and analysis of the raw data. AWS X-Ray complements this by tracing the execution flow across different components of the GenAI application, helping to visualize the ''reasoning path'' and identify where misinterpretations might occur within the application''s logic or external service calls. Option A (incorrect) uses Amazon DynamoDB, which is not optimized for storing large volumes of unstructured log data like detailed prompt inputs and outputs, and CloudWatch Metrics alone won''t capture the detailed reasoning path. Option C (incorrect) involves manual downloading and periodic analysis, which is inefficient and reactive, not suitable for systematic troubleshooting. Option D (incorrect) streams data to Amazon Redshift, which is an analytical data warehouse, but the primary need is detailed logging and tracing for debugging individual requests, not just aggregate analytics, and Redshift might be overkill for this specific troubleshooting task.', 68, 'm_68_2', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A GenAI application integrates with multiple external APIs for data enrichment, and users are reporting intermittent failures. The team needs to trace requests across these services to identify the exact point of failure and measure latency for each integration step. Which AWS service is best suited for this diagnostic task?', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch Logs to aggregate all application logs and manually search for error messages and timestamps across different services.","Implement Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to stream all application events to Amazon S3, then use Amazon Athena for querying the logs.","Leverage AWS X-Ray to provide end-to-end tracing of requests as they flow through the GenAI application and its external API calls, visualizing service maps and latency breakdowns.","Deploy custom agents on Amazon EC2 instances to collect performance metrics and send them to Amazon CloudWatch Metrics for dashboard visualization."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is specifically designed for end-to-end tracing across distributed applications and services. AWS X-Ray provides a visual service map, detailed trace data, and latency breakdowns for each segment of a request, including calls to external APIs. This allows the team to pinpoint the exact point of failure and measure latency for each integration step effectively. Option A (incorrect) relies on manual log searching in Amazon CloudWatch Logs, which is time-consuming and difficult for correlating events across multiple services for a single request. Option B (incorrect) streams data to S3 for querying with Athena, which is good for aggregate analysis but not for real-time, end-to-end tracing of individual requests. Option D (incorrect) focuses on collecting performance metrics, which is useful, but doesn''t provide the detailed request tracing and service map visualization that X-Ray offers for identifying failure points across integrations.', 68, 'm_68_2', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A development team is performing A/B testing on two different RAG configurations. They need to compare the generated outputs from both configurations against a ''golden dataset'' of expected responses to quantify differences in accuracy and relevance. Which approach is MOST suitable for this comparison?', '["Manually review a random sample of outputs from both RAG configurations and subjectively assess their accuracy and relevance against the golden dataset.","Deploy both RAG configurations to separate Amazon SageMaker Endpoints and rely on user feedback metrics from the application to determine the better configuration.","Use AWS Lambda to automate the process of generating outputs from both RAG configurations for a set of ''golden queries'', then apply automated metrics (e.g., ROUGE, BLEU, or LLM-as-a-judge) to compare these outputs against the golden dataset''s expected responses.","Store all generated outputs in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to query the data for keyword matching against the golden dataset."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most suitable approach for systematically quantifying differences in accuracy and relevance against a golden dataset. AWS Lambda can automate the generation of outputs from both RAG configurations for a predefined set of ''golden queries.'' Automated metrics like ROUGE (for summarization/relevance), BLEU (for translation/generation quality), or an LLM-as-a-judge can then be applied to compare these outputs against the expected responses in the golden dataset, providing objective and scalable evaluation. Option A (incorrect) is a manual and subjective process that is not scalable or efficient for continuous A/B testing and lacks objective quantification. Option B (incorrect) relies on user feedback, which is often indirect and can be noisy, and doesn''t directly compare against a golden dataset for accuracy and relevance. Option D (incorrect) uses Amazon S3 and Athena for keyword matching, which is a very simplistic and often insufficient method for assessing the nuanced accuracy and relevance of GenAI outputs compared to advanced metrics or LLM-as-a-judge.', 68, 'm_68_2', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A GenAI application uses a vector database (e.g., Amazon OpenSearch Service) for RAG, and users report that some queries return irrelevant information. The team suspects that the embeddings stored in the database are stale or misaligned with the current model. They need to validate the quality of the stored embeddings. Which approach is MOST effective?', '["Periodically re-index all documents in the vector database with the latest embedding model, assuming this resolves any misalignment.","Generate a ''golden dataset'' of queries with known relevant documents, re-embed these queries with the current model, and compare their similarity scores against the stored embeddings of the relevant documents in the vector database.","Monitor the latency of queries to the vector database using Amazon CloudWatch, assuming high latency indicates stale embeddings.","Implement an AWS Lambda function to randomly sample stored embeddings and visualize them using t-SNE, looking for visual clusters."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective approach for validating the quality and alignment of stored embeddings. By creating a ''golden dataset'' of queries with known relevant documents, and then re-embedding these queries with the *current* model, the team can directly compare the similarity scores against the *stored* embeddings of the relevant documents. This comparison will reveal if the stored embeddings are still effectively retrieving the correct documents, thus identifying staleness or misalignment. Option A (incorrect) is a brute-force solution that might resolve the issue but doesn''t provide a systematic way to *validate* the quality or detect the *cause* of misalignment. It''s a remediation, not a diagnostic. Option C (incorrect) monitors latency, which is a performance metric and not directly indicative of embedding quality or relevance. Option D (incorrect) visualizing embeddings can be helpful for general understanding but is not a quantitative or systematic method to validate the *retrieval effectiveness* or *alignment* of embeddings for specific queries.', 68, 'm_68_2', 19, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_64_all_1775003892874', 'A company is building a GenAI agent that performs multi-step reasoning. When the agent provides incorrect answers, the team needs a way to visualize and analyze the intermediate steps and tool calls made by the agent to pinpoint where the reasoning went wrong. Which AWS service combination is best for this ''reasoning path analysis''?', '["Store the final agent output in Amazon S3 and manually review it for errors, inferring the reasoning path from the output.","Use Amazon CloudWatch Metrics to monitor the agent''s overall success rate, and Amazon CloudWatch Alarms to notify of low performance.","Instrument the agent to log each intermediate thought, tool call, and observation to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, and use AWS X-Ray to trace the execution flow of the agent''s components and external interactions.","Deploy the agent on an Amazon EC2 instance and use a local debugger to step through the code during execution, capturing logs manually."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective approach for ''reasoning path analysis'' in multi-step GenAI agents. By instrumenting the agent to log each intermediate thought, tool call, and observation to Amazon CloudWatch Logs, the team captures the detailed sequence of the agent''s internal process. AWS X-Ray then complements this by tracing the execution flow across the agent''s components and any external services (tool calls), providing a visual representation of the entire reasoning path, making it easy to pinpoint where errors or missteps occurred. Option A (incorrect) only stores the final output, which is insufficient for understanding the multi-step reasoning process. Option B (incorrect) monitors aggregate success rates and performance, but does not provide the granular, step-by-step detail needed for debugging individual reasoning paths. Option D (incorrect) involves manual debugging on an EC2 instance, which is not scalable, automated, or suitable for post-hoc analysis of production issues.', 68, 'm_68_2', 20, NULL, NULL);

